    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Geologic surface interpolations can be augmented by adding physical constraints to available data. Here we outline a method that allows one to constrain surface interpolations for multiple geologic surfaces based on thickness of the bounded layer. Minimum curvature spline methods can be readily adapted to constrain the data by using Lagrange multiplier formulations. In simple two line interpolations for small slopes the method improves the solution substantially. In a field example at Raplee Ridge the power of this method is illustrated by overcoming interpenetration of two surfaces. The method can be extended to allow for higher gradients in elevation by adding additional terms to the governing biharmonic equation.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Kaven, J. Ole, Mazzeo, Rafe and Pollard, David D. (2006). Constraining Geologic Surface Interpolations Using Elastic Plate Bending Solutions With Applications to Raplee Ridge, Utah. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at: http://purl.stanford.edu/nv171gp2555",
